[Music]  india's festive season is underway as  millions of people gather for religious  festivals  often without any protective measures  against covet 19.  health experts fear these mass  gatherings could turn into super  spreader events  i can find many people are moving  without the mask they are not  maintaining the social distancing i am  feeling happy though the crowd is  excessive i am sweating like hell but  still i am enjoying it  [Music]  same time last year infection numbers  spiked with hundreds of thousands of new  cases each day  i'm ben fazoulen nice to have you along  anyone who's been to india knows  festivals mean masses of people and with  it a risk of infections spreading  after a brutal second wave and a bumpy  vaccination roll out close to a third of  the population is now fully vaccinated  that's enough for many in india to join  in the festivities  [Music]  the victory of good over evil  that is one of the key themes around the  hindu festivals of the shera and diwali  [Music]  and these delhi residents are clamoring  to leave the evil of the coronavirus  behind  celebrations are meant to be low-key  this is the only traditional theshara  performance allowed in the capital this  year  and the audience is strictly limited  but that isn't holding these crowds back  nor are people shying away from busy  festive markets  restrictions in shopping districts have  been lifted completely  and covert appropriate behavior isn't  being enforced  for many here their confidence stems  from the vaccine  i've gotten my second dose so now i feel  a lot more comfortable about being in  public places  cases are low right now so our hopes are  high and vaccinations are going well  for the lucky few who have been allowed  into performances like this one  it is the closest they felt to normalcy  since the pandemic began  i think more excited than scared because  it's been such a long long time that  we've been able to do anything like this  i miss the crowds  the food stalls and the swings but i'm  still really glad we got to attend this  the head of the delhi government also  reinforced a message of hope  i pray that we defeat the demon of the  karuna virus  that god keeps all of you healthy and  free of  disease lord rama kills the demon king  ravan  the euphoric crowds momentarily forget  about the pandem  but it may yet be a long wait till the  threat of the coronavirus 2 goes up in  flames  [Applause]  rajiv tascopter joins us now he heads  the center for social medicine and  community health at jnu in delhi nice to  have you on the show again  as we saw there celebrations underway  and  people having a great time but tell me  what what do you think only around 30  percent fully vaccinated how risky is  that for these people  hello ben  um  the visuals of the durga puja that we  saw  particularly if you talk of west bengal  which has certainly witnessed a rise in  cases  the the outcome of that surge would  depend on an interplay of two things  uh these the zero prevalence the zero  positivity in west bengal by the last  fourth national serial survey  which is relatively low at about 61  and coverage with two vaccines which is  also relatively low at about 27  so a state like west bengal with both  the parameters being low would certainly  be at additional risk what could perhaps  be protective and that's something we  would be able to gauge full in the  coming weeks  is how much protection a single dose of  vaccine is according because west bengal  has uh about 72 coverage with a single  dose  and that would really um  entail what proportion would actually  fall sick  from the covid and how much this current  surge would last  so where would you put the likelihood of  another surge of cases then  given the very high levels of zero  positivity  in most indian states and in fact the  more recent ones which have come out in  the last two weeks state-specific ones  actually show that zero protection is  pretty high as a combination of  both vaccination as well as disease  both clinical disease and asymptomatic  disease so that's protective uh  presently but should there be a new  variant or particularly a new highly  transmissible variant that could  certainly change the complexion of the  game  and and what role might natural immunity  play now that so many indians have  caught the coronavirus  well cases are currently at a low of  about 12  000 to 13 000.  the deaths may seem a bit  disproportionately high  uh compared to the new cases but that's  because several states have uh have  reviewed the data reconciled the death  date and therefore there have been some  new death  notifications but they are not new  deaths uh so to say  so therefore at present it's seemingly a  comfortable situation uh but that that  owes a lot to the to the high zero  positivity and we heard in that report  uh  a confident young man saying that lots  of people have now been vaccinated i  mean  we're talking about what you said 27 30  um and india recently administering its  one billionth coronavirus jab i mean are  people getting overconfident  um the one million jab is certainly a  very creditable  feat  i think what we need to be worried about  is roughly half a dozen to eight states  which are below the national average  these are very large states states with  very large populations  one of one of the largest states uttar  pradesh for example has  uh 20 percent of the population covered  with two doses a state with high  incidence maharashtra which which has  mumbai  city in it uh has a coverage  of  32 with two vaccines  so so these are the states that we need  to look out for on the other hand the  states which have really had high  coverage above 50  and nearly 60 percent in some of the  smaller states so it's a very mixed  picture we really need to keep a look  out for some of these very large states  rajib one thing that india hasn't been  battling is uh vaccine hesitancy a lot  of countries are struggling with that  why is india doing so well in in getting  people to actually take the vaccine  we need to give credit to  perhaps two things  one certainly  the  the forthright conviction of the present  government  in putting its full weight uh in the  vaccination program  uh  and but secondly what we also need to  pay tribute to is a systematic  strengthening of the immunization  program in the context of  polio eradication and this current phase  of strengthening or systematically  strengthening has been going on for last  two decades  once the 2000 milestone for polio  eradication was missed  so the current vaccination has  the current campaign has come on very  strong fundamentals and that's what is  really paying uh paying dividends it  isn't that there haven't been cases of  hesitancy but the campaign has also been  proactive about it but uh but we  certainly need to recognize the strong  fundamentals  project  from jnu thank you very much for being  on the show today  thank you  another viewer question now from derek  williams and this time a question on the  covert 19 vaccine  why do i need a kovit covit-19 vaccine  if it doesn't prevent me from catching  the disease  i think that this question more than any  other illustrates how governments and  healthcare authorities have really  struggled with messaging in this  pandemic um to answer it let's go back  over some of the stuff that they could  have probably communicated better  first i think  is the fact that despite really great  efficacy numbers from clinical trials  the vaccines that we have are not  perfect silver bullets and we should  never have expected them to be what they  have so far proved to provide  is some very effective short-term  protection from serious illness and  death  caused by covid um it would be great  if that protection were indefinite but  but there are indications that it might  be less long-term  than we'd hoped  one recent model based study that's  gotten some attention  posits that if sars cov2 becomes endemic  then reinfection in people who got it  once  could on average occur again in just 16  months now if correct  that obviously has implications for  durability of immune response in  vaccinated people as well  but that's just one prediction and over  the course of this pandemic  it feels like those have proven wrong a  lot more often than they've proven right  so  i would encourage people to focus more  on what we really do  know now um  like the crystal clear data-based  statistics published by the cdc in the  u.s  they showed that although vaccines  haven't prevented covid19 or it's spread  completely there  unvaccinated people are still five times  more likely to test positive for the  disease  than vaccinated people are and  more importantly unvaccinated people are  10 times more likely to end up in the  hospital and 10 times more likely to die  so  does being vaccinated guarantee you  won't get coveted 19 or or maybe even  die from it no  but for at least a while maybe a long  while  vaccines certainly do  stack the deck a lot more in your favor  and you saw the celebrations from india  earlier  let's leave you with preparations for  sunday's day of the dead parade in  mexico city it was cancelled last year  due to the pandemic  authorities only gave the go ahead at  short notice artisans rushing to put  together their floats and costumes and  masks the parade is not an ancient  tradition it was actually inspired by  the 2015 james bond movie spectre the  ideas caught on last time around three  million people took part  i'm ben fazel and thanks for joining us  on another coconutting special  [Music]

